Currently, IValue (aten/src/ATen/core/ivalue.h) has the following templated constructors:
    template<class T>
    IValue(c10::List<T> v);
    template<class T>
    IValue(at::ArrayRef<T> v);
    template<class T>
    IValue(const std::vector<T>& v);

These templated constructors recursively make use of the IValue(T) constructor, for whatever selection of what T is. Unfortunately, these constructors are not SFINAE'd, which means we will consider IValue constructible for any setting of T, even if the underlying IValue(T) constructor doesn't exist. This has lead to the ugly code here:
  // Assume T is decayed
  template <typename T>
  using not_ok_to_box =
    guts::disjunction<
      guts::negation<
        guts::disjunction<
          std::is_constructible<IValue, T>,
          // TensorOptions are not directly constructible into IValue,
          // but torch::jit::push knows how to handle them
          std::is_same<TensorOptions, T>,
          // void returns are ok
          std::is_same<void, T>
        >>
      ,   
      // some constructors are templated (and therefore pass
      // is_constructible), but do not actually work with all 
      // template arguments, so we must blacklist them explicitly
      // TODO: The correct fix is to sfinae based on is_constructible of T
      std::is_same<optional<ArrayRef<at::Dimname>>, T>,
      std::is_same<ArrayRef<at::Dimname>, T>

    >;

The purpose of this task is to appropriately SFINAE these constructors (and any other relevant constructors) with a test that IValue is constructible from T, so that the not_ok_to_box template can be simplified by removing the explicit tests for Dimname. You'll know you've done it right when everything compiles.
